{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_vgqs6dsnkk54z6zndthi2zgscsdlvowxp7zkkuusfsxrwhe6nwiq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. **The Rust translation is byte-identical to the C across every configuration and error path tested.**\n\n## Results\n\n**Phase A artifacts** (in `translation/`):\n- `SYMBOLS.md` — 4 exported symbols (`printLine`, `bad`, `good`, `driver`); symbol diff **empty**. The two `static` C helpers (`helperGood`, `helperBad`) are correctly kept out of the Rust dynsym too.\n- `ERRORS.md` — 6 rows. The C has *no* return codes, enums, asserts, or range checks (all functions are `void`); the entire error surface is the single `if (line != NULL)` guard, plus generic FFI boundaries.\n- `CONFIGS.md` — 14 rows. No runtime options exist, so the axes are entry point × input shape × call sequence.\n\n**Test suite** — 27 tests, all passing. Both `.so`s are loaded via `libloading` and driven only through exported C symbols; stdout is compared byte-for-byte.\n- Phase B: 14/14 rows, ~1900 randomized inputs (seeded), incl. all 255 single-byte strings, high-bit/non-UTF-8 payloads, format-specifier payloads, lengths to 65535, and a 200-op randomized interleaved script.\n- Phase C: 6/6 rows + generic boundaries (NULL, empty, embedded NUL, interior-offset pointers, oversized, out-of-range args through a widened FFI signature).\n- Phase D: symbol parity, no unexpected undefined symbols, both import the same `puts@GLIBC`. All configs pass in both `release` and `debug`; no `[features]` exist, so `--no-default-features` is the only other combo. No binary target exists in either project, so there is no binary stdout to compare (asserted by a test so the claim can't silently go stale).\n\n## Two things worth flagging\n\n1. **No divergences were found — the translation was already correct.** The only Rust change I made was adding `libloading`/`libc` dev-dependencies.\n\n2. **Initial failures were harness bugs, not translation bugs.** Capturing stdout by redirecting fd 1 pulled libtest's own progress text (\"`test foo ... ok`\") into the captures. Fixed by flushing Rust's `stdout`/`stderr` before `dup2` and forcing single-threaded execution via `translation/.cargo/config.toml`; `capture()` now asserts `RUST_TEST_THREADS=1` so it fails loudly rather than flakily.\n\nI also **mutation-tested the harness** to confirm it isn't vacuously passing: removing the NULL guard → SIGSEGV, and swapping the two calls in `good()` → 5 failures. Both mutations were reverted (`md5` verified). Note that a plain `mv` restore left cargo with a stale `.so` and produced a spurious failure — the run above forces a rebuild.\n\nRe-run everything with `cd translation && ./run_all_configs.sh`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1171,"cache_read_input_tokens":78506,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1171,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"13fea489-d404-4a96-804e-edf2e33662f1","uuid":"b8b6ccd8-f6f0-4753-abf9-032559024f2b","timestamp":"2026-09-05T03:36:48.608Z"}
